        <div class="service-detail-img">
          <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1579684385127-1ef15d508118?w=800&q=80" alt="Undiagnosed Facial Pains treatment at Dr. Ravi's Clinic" loading="lazy">
        </div>
        <div class="service-detail-content">
          <p>Chronic facial pain with no clear diagnosis is one of the most frustrating conditions a patient can face. You've seen dentists, ENT specialists, neurologists — and still have no answers. At Dr. Ravi's Dental and Orofacial Pain Clinic, we specialize in diagnosing and treating complex facial pain syndromes that others have been unable to explain.</p><h3>Conditions We Diagnose & Treat</h3><ul><li><strong>Persistent Idiopathic Facial Pain (Atypical Facial Pain):</strong> Continuous, dull, aching pain without identifiable cause, often crossing traditional nerve boundaries</li><li><strong>Burning Mouth Syndrome:</strong> A burning sensation on the tongue, palate, or lips with no visible abnormalities</li><li><strong>Trigeminal Neuropathy:</strong> Numbness, tingling, or constant aching in trigeminal nerve distribution after dental procedures or trauma</li><li><strong>Referred Pain:</strong> Pain originating from the cervical spine, sinuses, or intracranial structures that presents as facial pain</li><li><strong>Neuropathic Pain:</strong> Nerve damage causing shooting, burning, or electric sensations</li></ul><h3>Why Facial Pain Is Often Misdiagnosed</h3><p>Facial pain overlaps multiple medical specialties — dentistry, neurology, ENT, and pain medicine. Each specialist evaluates within their domain, but orofacial pain specialists are uniquely trained to bridge these disciplines. We understand the complex neuroanatomy of the face and the interplay between dental, muscular, and neurological pain mechanisms.</p><h3>Our Diagnostic Protocol</h3><p><strong>Step 1 — Rule Out Dental Causes:</strong> Comprehensive examination and imaging to eliminate decay, infection, cracked teeth, and occlusal problems.</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Neurological Assessment:</strong> Testing for trigeminal nerve function, allodynia (pain from normally non-painful stimuli), and hyperalgesia.</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Quantitative Sensory Testing:</strong> Measuring thermal and mechanical detection thresholds to identify neuropathic components.</p><p><strong>Step 4 — Multidisciplinary Review:</strong> Coordinating with ENT, neurology, and pain medicine when indicated.</p><h3>Treatment Approaches</h3><p>Depending on the diagnosis, we may use topical medications, systemic neuromodulators, nerve blocks for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, cognitive behavioral therapy referrals, and coordinated multidisciplinary care. Our goal is not just pain relief — it's restoring your quality of life.</p>
          <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
            <div class="faq-item"><button type="button" class="faq-question">I've seen 5 doctors and still have no diagnosis. Can you help? <span class="faq-icon"><i class="fas fa-chevron-down"></i></span></button><div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes. Orofacial pain is a recognized dental specialty, and our training specifically addresses the gaps between dentistry and medicine. We have successfully diagnosed many patients who had been suffering for years.</p></div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><button type="button" class="faq-question">Is this pain 'all in my head'? <span class="faq-icon"><i class="fas fa-chevron-down"></i></span></button><div class="faq-answer"><p>Absolutely not. All pain is real and has a physiological basis. Even when imaging is normal, changes in nerve signaling and central sensitization are measurable and treatable.</p></div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><button type="button" class="faq-question">How long does it take to get a diagnosis? <span class="faq-icon"><i class="fas fa-chevron-down"></i></span></button><div class="faq-answer"><p>Some cases are diagnosed in a single visit. Complex cases may require 2-3 visits with specialized testing. We communicate clearly at every step so you understand your condition.</p></div></div>
